<title>BRAZIL: Cold front heads for key Brazil soybean area-Somar.</title>
<headline>Cold front heads for key Brazil soybean area-Somar.</headline>
<dateline>SAO PAULO 1997-02-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>Rains are set to continue in Brazil's key southern soybean growing regions with a cold front heading for Rio Grande do Sul state Feb 19-20, said independent meteorologists Somar.</p>
<p>In Parana state, sporadic summer rains will continue with high temperatures throughout the region, the weather forecaster said in a weekly report.</p>
<p>In the center-west, isolated rains are forecast to maintain soil moisture in key producing regions.</p>
<p>In the first half of the month, rains returned to Rio Grande do Sul, replenishing soil moisture in the north-east which had become dry, Somar said. In Parana, rains were excessive in isolated spots, exceeding 125 mm in some cases.</p>
<p>&quot;These rains have already caused damage to the soybean crop due to the excess soil moisture and some floods,&quot; the report said.</p>
<p>Rainfall in the past week and the historical monthly average in key states in millimeters were as follows.</p>
<p>			TOTAL RAIN   TOTAL RAIN   AVERAGE</p>
<p>			 Feb 14-17    Feb 1-17    February</p>
<p> Londrina (PR)	    66.2	  253.2	 180</p>
<p> Cascaval (PR)	    23.0	  148.5	 175</p>
<p> Passo Fundo (RS)	 19.0	  112.4	 147</p>
<p> Sao Luiz Gonzaga (RS)  41.4	  119.9	 122</p>
<p> Santa Maria (RS)	  9.3	  118.0	 141</p>
<p> Mato Grosso		45.0	  138.0	 240</p>
<p>--Joelle Diderich, Sao Paulo newsroom +55 11 248 5417</p>
